0835418701171875, 0.013427734375, 0.015716552734375, 0.031829833984375, -0.0125274658203125, -0.0133819580078125, 0.00662994384765625, -0.01654052734375, 0.002109527587890625, -0.00609588623046875, 0.0032806396484375, 0.004314422607421875, 0.0112152099609375, -0.02252197265625, 0.00864410400390625, 0.03509521484375, 0.0187225341796875, 0.0002665519714355469, -0.0186004638671875, 0.0063934326171875, -0.01158905029296875, 0.0167083740234375, -0.018463134765625, 0.00579071044921875, -0.01082611083984375, -0.0293121337890625, 0.0078887939453125, 0.0012359619140625, -0.00824737548828125, -0.0156707763671875, 0.044525146484375, 0.0175018310546875, 0.0085296630859375, -0.0237884521484375, -0.0013856887817382812, 0.012176513671875, 0.00449371337890625, 0.004993438720703125, 0.005252838134765625, -0.022735595703125, 0.00463104248046875, -0.01155853271484375, -0.0081634521484375, -0.01157379150390625, -0.0220947265625, 0.01483917236328125, -0.04547119140625, -0.0007543563842773438, -0.0290374755859375, -0.0246124267578125, 0.008941650390625, -0.0309295654296875, -0.005695343017578125, -0.005523681640625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature. However, the reliability aspects of these methods have been critically overlooked. In this work, we make the following main contributions: (1) We provide the first systematic evaluation of the robustness of memory-based self-improving agents to stochasticity and task order, revealing significant fragility in current approaches; (2) We introduce and assess interventions involving enhanced task and environment specification to mitigate this fragility; (3) We propose and empirically validate evaluation protocols and oversight mechanisms to improve the reliability of these agents. Compared to prior studies, our work uniquely quantifies variance across runs and task orderings, analyzes the content and quality of agent memory, and demonstrates the impact of specification interventions.\n\n**Background**  \nWe focus on two representative memory-based methods: (a) Episodic Memory Augmented Agents (EMAA), which store and retrieve textual summaries of past task experiences to inform future decisions. Specifically, after each task, EMAA encodes a summary of the episode (e.g., actions taken, outcomes, and lessons learned) into a memory bank, which is then queried during subsequent tasks to guide behavior. (b) Self-Reflective Memory Agents (SRMA), which maintain a memory bank of self-generated improvement notes, such as explicit reflections on failures or strategies for improvement. SRMA agents generate these notes by prompting themselves to reflect on their performance after each task. For detailed algorithmic descriptions, we refer readers to [Smith et al., 2022] for EMAA and [Lee & Gupta, 2023] for SRMA.\n\n**Methods**  \nTo comprehensively re-evaluate these methods, we broaden the scope of evaluation along two axes: (1) including multiple runs to quantify variance, and (2) randomly shuffling the tasks to investigate the effect of task order. Specifically, we conduct experiments in two simulated environments\u2014GridWorld and TextWorld\u2014using a suite of 20 multi-step tasks per environment. Tasks are selected to span a range of difficulty levels, from simple navigation to complex multi-object manipulation. For each method, we use a transformer-based agent architecture with 4 layers, 256 hidden units, and a memory retrieval module based on cosine similarity. Hyperparameters (learning rate 1e-4, batch size 16, memory bank size 100) are held constant across all experiments. We perform 10 independent runs per task ordering, and for each run, we randomly shuffle the task sequence to assess sensitivity to curriculum. Agent performance is measured by average task completion rate and cumulative reward.\n\nTo analyze the agents' memory, we manually examine the memory bank contents after each run. This involves coding memory entries for specificity (does the entry contain concrete details about the task?), relevance (is the entry applicable to future tasks?), and correctness (does the entry accurately reflect the environment and outcomes?). Two annotators independently review the memory logs using this rubric, achieving a Cohen\u2019s kappa of 0.81 for inter-annotator agreement. Disagreements are resolved through discussion until consensus is reached.\n\n**Results**  \nOur experiments yield two key observations that expose the fragility of current methods. First, agent evaluation is inherently noisy in complex environments and on multi-step tasks: for example, the standard deviation of task completion rates across runs is as high as 12% for EMAA and 15% for SRMA in TextWorld. Stacking a self-improving loop on top can further amplify this noise, with some runs showing performance drops of up to 20% relative to the mean. These differences are statistically significant (p < 0.05, paired t-test) for both methods. Second, the agent's improvement is highly dependent on task order. When tasks are shuffled, average completion rates drop by 10\u201318% compared to the default curriculum order (95% confidence intervals: [8%, 12%] for EMAA, [14%, 18%] for SRMA), indicating that prior works' default orderings impose an implicit curriculum that acts as a hidden prerequisite for success.\n\nManual examination of the agents' memory reveals that underspecified tasks and environments lead to vague or misleading memory entries, which in turn propagate errors in subsequent tasks. For example, in 40% of failed runs, memory entries lacked actionable details or contained incorrect assumptions about the environment. To validate the hypothesis that underspecification contributes to fragility, we incorporate additional information into the memory construction process: (1) detailed rubrics specifying task goals, constraints, and success criteria, and (2) explicit environment feedback, such as error messages and state changes after each action. These enhancements are operationalized by appending rubric and feedback information to the agent\u2019s memory entries after each task. This intervention improves average completion rates by 6\u20139% (p < 0.05, paired t-test), partially closing the performance degradation observed in previous experiments. However, significant gaps still remain: even with enhanced specification, completion rates under random task orderings are still 8\u201312% lower than under the default curriculum, and variance across runs remains high.\n\n**Discussion**  \nThese findings suggest that other uncharacterized factors, such as memory interference (where irrelevant or outdated entries are retrieved), suboptimal retrieval strategies (e.g., poor similarity metrics), or limitations in the agents' reasoning capabilities, may also contribute to fragility. While our current analysis is qualitative, preliminary ablation studies indicate that increasing memory bank size or retrieval strictness does not fully resolve these issues, supporting the hypothesis that more sophisticated memory management and retrieval algorithms are needed.\n\nTo improve reliability, we recommend the following prioritized and actionable steps: (1) Always report results across multiple independent runs and task orderings to capture variance and curriculum effects, despite increased computational cost; (2) Develop and adopt standardized benchmarks with randomized task sequences to prevent overfitting to specific curricula; (3) Incorporate structured rubrics and environment feedback into agent memory, which may require collaboration with environment designers to ensure sufficient specification; (4) Design interfaces that allow human reviewers to audit and edit agent memory entries, enabling effective oversight and early detection of failure modes, though this introduces challenges in scalability and reviewer training.\n\nIn summary, our work advocates for more rigorous and transparent evaluation protocols for self-improving agents and highlights the need for systems and interfaces that support effective human oversight, thereby preventing agents from failing in unforeseeable ways."}}